                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi C
                  %mo :
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_C : S \to C
            along the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ↪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \hookrightarrow [S^{op},V]
            (i.e. the
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+extension"} Yoneda extension
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi S
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi S
                                %mi C
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇓
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↗
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                              %mo −
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mi op
                          %mo ,
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  S &amp;\stackrel{S_C}{\to}&amp;&amp; C
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{Y} &amp;\Downarrow&amp; \nearrow_{|-|}
                  \\\
                  [S^{op},V]
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            If we assume that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copower"} tensored
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            formula for left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
            we find that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in [S^{op}, V]
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi s
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  |X| \simeq
                  \\int^{s \in S}
                  S_C(s) \cdot X_s
                  \\,.
          %p
            For instance when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo =
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = \Delta
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            this reads more recognizably
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Δ
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  |X| \simeq
                  \\int^{[n] \in \Delta}
                  \\Delta_C[n] \cdot X_n
                  \\,.
          %p
            The corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            operation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  N :
                  C \stackrel{}{\to} [S^{op},V]
          %p
            is given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/restricted+Yoneda+embedding"} restricted Yoneda embedding
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi op
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi S
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi V
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  N(c) : S^{op} \stackrel{S_C^{op}}{\to} C^{op}
                  \\stackrel{C(-,c)}{\to}
                  V
                  \\,.
          %p
          #NerveAndRealizationAreAdjoint.num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              Nerve and realization are a pair of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    {|-|} \,\dashv\, N
            %p
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            %p
          (
          %a{:href => "#Kan58"}> Kan 1958, Sec. 3
          )
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Using the fact that the Hom in its first argument sends
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coends"} coends
              to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ends"} ends
              and then using the definition of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copower"} tensoring
              over
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              = succeed ":" do
                %a{:href => "adjoint+functor#InTermsOfHomIsomorphism"} hom-isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi Hom
                              %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mo ,
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≔
                            %msub
                              %mi Hom
                              %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mo ∫
                              %mi s
                            %msub
                              %mi S
                              %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi s
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⋅
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mi s
                            %mo ,
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≃
                            %msub
                              %mo ∫
                              %mi s
                            %msub
                              %mi Hom
                              %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi S
                              %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi s
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⋅
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mi s
                            %mo ,
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≃
                            %msub
                              %mo ∫
                              %mi s
                            %msub
                              %mi Hom
                              %mi V
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mi s
                            %mo ,
                            %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi S
                              %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi s
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ,
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %msub
                              %mo ∫
                              %mi s
                            %msub
                              %mi Hom
                              %mi V
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mi s
                            %mo ,
                            %mi N
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi c
                            %msub
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mi s
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≃
                            %msub
                              %mi Hom
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                                %msup
                                  %mi S
                                  %mi op
                                %mo ,
                                %mi V
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo ,
                            %mi N
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    Hom_C(|X|, c)
                    \&amp;
                    \\coloneqq
                    Hom_C( \int^{s} S_C(s) \cdot X_s, c)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \simeq
                    \\int_{s} Hom_C( S_C(s) \cdot X_s, c)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \simeq
                    \\int_{s} Hom_V( X_s , C(S_C(s), c))
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    \\int_{s} Hom_V( X_s , N(c)_s)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \simeq
                    Hom_{[S^{op},V]}(X, N(c))
                    \\,,
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p
              where in the last step we used the definition of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functor+category"} enriched functor category
              in terms of an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/end"} end
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              In many cases we have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V =
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copower"} tensoring
              of an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              over a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              is given by coproducts as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi I
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %mi c
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    c \cdot I = \coprod_{i \in I} c
                    \\,.
            %p
              This is the case for instance for the below examples of realization of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#TopologicalRealizationOfSimplicialSets Topological realization of simplicial sets
          %p
            A classical example is given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+topological+space"} cosimplicial topological space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Top
                  %mo :
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta_{Top} : \Delta \to Top
          %p
            that sends the abstract
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]
            to the standard topological
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi Top
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_{Top}[n] \subset \mathbb{R}^n
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The corresponding realization is what is traditionally called
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
                of simplicial sets.
              %p
                By restricting this to simplicial sets which are themselves simplicial nerves of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
                (see
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "NervesOfCategories"} below
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-categories"} quasi-categories
              %p
                The construction generalizes also to a notion of
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization+of+simplicial+topological+spaces"} geometric realization of simplicial topological spaces
            %li
              %p
                The corresponding
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+simplicial+complex"} singular simplicial complex
                functor, producing the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} fundamental ∞-groupoid
                of a topological space.
          %p
            This topological nerve and realization adjunction plays a central role as a presentation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} model structure on topological spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
          %h3#NervesOfCategories Nerve and realization of categories
          %p
            Pretty much every notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category
            comes, or should come, with its canonical notion of simplicial nerve, induced from a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi C
                  %mo :
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %mi n
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta_C : \Delta \to n Cat
          %p
            that sends the standard
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
            to something like the free
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+n-graph"} directed graph
            underlying that simplex.
          %p
            For ordinary categories see the discussion at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization+of+categories"} geometric realization of categories
          %p
            One formalization of this for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = \infty
            in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-categories"} strict ∞-categories
            is the cosimplicial
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientals"} orientals
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi ω
                  %mo :
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ω
                  %mi Cat
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta_{\omega} : \Delta \to \omega Cat
                  \\,.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The induced
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
                is the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-nerve"} ∞-nerve
            %li
              %p
                The induced realization operation is the operation of forming the free
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ω
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oriental"} oriental
                for more details.
          %h3#doldkan_correspondence Dold–Kan correspondence
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            is the nerve/realization adjunction for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mo •
                  %mo :
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta_{C_\bullet} : \Delta \to Ch_+
          %p
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} category of chain complexes
            of abelian groups, which sends the standard
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
            to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            chain complex, more precisely to its normalized
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore complex
          %ul
            %li
              The induced realization is the normalized
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore complex
              functor extended from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
              to all
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
          %h3#higher_lie_integration__smooth_sullivan_construction Higher Lie integration / smooth Sullivan construction
          %p
            see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            %a{:href => "Lie+integration#LieIntegrationIsRightQuillenFunctor"} this Prop.
          %h3#simplicial_models_for_categories
            Simplicial models for
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            The canonical cosimplicial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicially+enriched+category"} simplicially enriched category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %mi SSet
                  %mtext -
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta \to SSet\text{-}Cat
          %p
            induces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+coherent+nerve"} homotopy coherent nerve
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SSet"} SSet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched categories
            and establishes the relation between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
            and the simplicially enriched model for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-categories
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+quasi-categories+and+simplicial+categories"}> relation between quasi-categories and simplicial categories
              \.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#FullAndFaithfulness Full and faithfulness
          %p
            When the nerve functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi C
                  %mo :
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_C : S \to C
            is said to be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+functor"} dense functor
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+with+arities"} monad with arities
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"}> nerve
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+with+arities"} monad with arities
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totalization"} totalization
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The notion of nerve and realization (not with these names yet) was introduced and proven to be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            in section 3 of
          %ul
            %li#Kan58
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Kan"}> Daniel Kan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Functors involving c.s.s complexes
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/1993103"} jstor:1993103
          %p
            In fact, in that very article apparently what is now called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
            is first discussed.
          %p
            Also, in that article, as an example of the general mechanism, also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            was found and discussed, independently of the work by Dold and Puppe shortly before, who used a much less general-nonsense approach.
          %p
            In an article in 1984, Dwyer and Kan look at this ‘nerve-realization’ context from a different viewpoint, using the term ‘singular functor’ where the above has used ‘nerve’. Their motivation example is that in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+category"} orbit category
            of a group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "-space:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W.+G.+Dwyer"} W. G. Dwyer
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D.+M.+Kan"} D. M. Kan
              = succeed "," do
                %em Singular functors and realization functors
          %p
            We should also mention the treatment in Leinster’s book and the relation to the notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subcategory"} dense subcategory
            or adequate subcategory in the sense of Isbell.
          %p
            In a
            %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2010/02/sheaves_do_not_belong_to_algeb.html"} blog post
            on the n-Category Café, Tom Leinster illustrates that “sections of a bundle” is a nerve operation, and its corresponding geometric realization is the construction of the étalé space of a presheaf.
